## Building an Automatic Grader

Functions with `if/elif/else` inside them can map any input to a category — perfect for grading.

### Letter Grade Converter

```xiom
fn letter_grade(score: Int) -> Str {
  if score >= 90 {
    return "A";
  } else if score >= 80 {
    return "B";
  } else if score >= 70 {
    return "C";
  } else if score >= 60 {
    return "D";
  } else {
    return "F";
  }
}

fn main() -> Int {
  io.println(letter_grade(95));  // A
  io.println(letter_grade(83));  // B
  io.println(letter_grade(55));  // F
  return 0;
}
```

### Pass/Fail Check

```xiom
fn is_passing(score: Int) -> Bool {
  return score >= 60;
}

fn is_honors(score: Int) -> Bool {
  return score >= 90;
}

fn main() -> Int {
  io.println(is_passing(72));   // true
  io.println(is_honors(88));    // false
  return 0;
}
```

### Full Grade Report

```xiom
fn grade_report(name: Str, score: Int) {
  io.println("Student: " + name);
  io.println("Score: ");
  io.println(score);
  io.println("Grade: " + letter_grade(score));
  if is_passing(score) {
    io.println("Status: PASS");
  } else {
    io.println("Status: FAIL");
  }
}

fn main() -> Int {
  grade_report("Kilo", 87);
  return 0;
}
```

Notice the order of `if/elif` checks in `letter_grade`: checking `>= 90` BEFORE `>= 80` ensures scores like 95 don't accidentally get a B. Always check the highest threshold first!

